 ## Overview
-Texture-Based Volume Rendering (TBVR) is a direct volume ray casting method that accelerates rendering by mapping volumetric data into GPU texture memory and sampling it via rasterized proxy geometry (parallel planes) [S1, S2, S7, unofficial]. Unlike ray marching which computes samples per pixel, TBVR projects slices through the volume onto the screen, leveraging the graphics pipeline's hardware interpolation and alpha blending to composite the image [S2, unofficial][S9]. It is distinguished from [2D Visualization from 3D Grid (Slow)](1507-2d-visualization-from-3d-grid-slow.md) by its use of GPU texture units for interpolation, and from [Raymarching with 3D Volume Texture](10839-raymarching-with-3d-volume-texture.md) by its object-order (forward projection) approach using explicit geometry rather than image-order ray loops [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Texture-Based Volume Rendering (TBVR) is a direct volume rendering technique that accelerates image-order ray casting by decomposing the 3D volume into a set of 2D planar slices, treating them as proxy geometry primitives (polygons) that are rasterized by the GPU [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software-based ray casting by leveraging the graphics pipeline's texture sampling and hardware alpha blending to perform compositing in parallel [S1, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. Unlike pure ray marching which computes samples per pixel, TBVR computes samples per slice vertex, making it significantly faster on older hardware but historically prone to sampling artifacts when viewing angles deviate from slice normals [S1, unofficial][S3, book].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A 3D scalar volume $V$ (regular grid), camera parameters (view/projection matrices), transfer function $T$, and lighting configuration.
-*   **Output:** A 2D image frame buffer.
+*   **Input:** A 3D scalar volume dataset (regular grid), a camera position and orientation, and a transfer function (mapping scalar values to color/opacity).
+*   **Output:** A 2D color image composited in the frame buffer.
 
