 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Object-Aligned Artifacts:** Using 2D texture stacks requires replicating data in 3 directions, increasing memory by 3x [S2, unofficial][S3, book]. Switching between stacks causes "popping" artifacts and intensity variations because the sampling distance changes with the viewpoint [S2, S6, unofficial][S9].
-*   **Viewing Angle Artifacts:** With 2D textures, viewing angles near 45 degrees to the slice plane cause artifacts as the viewer sees between slice gaps [S1, S8, unofficial].
-*   **Sampling Rate Dependency:** The rendering quality depends on the volume's native resolution. Low resolution leads to aliasing. This is mitigated by adding intermediate slices and using trilinear filtering [S3, book][S7, unofficial][S9].
-*   **Coordinate System Rotation:** If the model is rotated (e.g., y-axis to z-axis), clipping plane logic must be adjusted to match the transformed texture coordinates [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Trilinear interpolation in 3D textures can cause blurring if the volume resolution is low. [S1, unofficial]
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** Texture coordinates must be clamped or wrapped correctly to avoid sampling outside the volume bounds. [S5, unofficial]
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the camera is parallel to a slice plane, the slice may degenerate to a line or point, causing rendering artifacts. [S2, unofficial]
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### 2D Slice Replication Approach
-A variant where the volume is stored as three stacks of 2D textures (aligned with X, Y, Z axes) [S9]. Proxy geometry consists of simple rectangles aligned with these stacks [S9]. **Difference:** It is faster to render than view-aligned 3D textures but suffers from 3x memory consumption due to data replication and "popping" artifacts when the camera view changes direction [S2, unofficial][S9]. **When to choose:** On older hardware lacking 3D texture support or when performance is critical and view changes are limited [S1, S8, unofficial].
+Stores volume as three stacks of 2D textures (X, Y, Z aligned). Proxy geometry is simple rectangles. **Pros:** Fast, bilinear interpolation. **Cons:** 3x memory usage, data replication, "popping" artifacts when switching stacks, intensity variations as camera moves. **When to use:** Legacy hardware, fixed viewpoints. [S2, unofficial][S3, book][S9]
 
-### View-Aligned Slices
-A variant where proxy geometry planes are always orthogonal to the viewing direction, sampled from a single 3D texture [S2, S7, unofficial]. **Difference:** It eliminates the 45-degree viewing artifacts and intensity variations of object-aligned slices, providing consistent sampling distance [S2, S7, unofficial]. **When to choose:** For interactive applications requiring free camera rotation and high image quality, leveraging modern GPU 3D texture support [S1, S7, unofficial].
+### View-Aligned Slicing
+Slices are generated orthogonal to the viewing direction. **Pros:** Consistent sampling distance, no popping artifacts, better image quality. **Cons:** Slower geometry generation, requires 3D textures. **When to use:** Interactive applications with arbitrary camera movement. [S2, unofficial][S3, book]
+
+### Object-Aligned Slicing
+Slices are aligned with the principal axes of the volume. **Pros:** Simple geometry, fast. **Cons:** Artifacts at oblique angles, requires 3D texture stacks or replication. **When to use:** Orthographic views, static scenes. [S2, unofficial][S6, unofficial]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of proxy slices and $P$ is the number of pixels covered by the slices. The complexity is independent of the volume resolution $D_x D_y D_z$ for sampling (handled by hardware interpolation), but dependent on the number of slices chosen for quality [S3, book][S7, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(D_x D_y D_z)$ for 3D textures. $O(3 \cdot D_x D_y D_z)$ for 2D texture stacks [S2, unofficial][S3, book].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive frame rates on modern GPUs by offloading interpolation and compositing to hardware [S1, S7, unofficial]. 3D textures are slightly slower than 2D bilinear interpolation but provide superior quality [S1, S8, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High performance on consumer hardware; simple implementation using standard graphics pipeline; good image quality with 3D textures [S1, S2, S7, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Memory intensive for large volumes (especially 2D stacks); artifacts at oblique angles with 2D textures; sampling distance varies with view in object-aligned modes [S2, S6, S8, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of moderate-sized volumes on GPUs [S1, S7, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Extremely large volumes that exceed GPU memory; scenes requiring precise control over every sample point (use ray marching instead) [S2, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M)$, where $N$ is the number of slices and $M$ is the number of fragments per slice. The complexity is independent of the number of pixels, unlike ray marching. [S3, book]
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for 3D textures, where $V$ is the volume size. $O(3V)$ for 2D texture stacks. [S2, unofficial][S3, book]
+*   **Measured Performance:** On modern GPUs, TBVR achieves interactive frame rates (30+ FPS) for volumes up to $512^3$ with 3D textures. 2D texture approaches are faster on older hardware but suffer from quality degradation. [S1, unofficial][S3, book]
+*   **Strengths:** High performance on GPUs, simple implementation, leverages hardware blending. [S1, unofficial][S3, book]
+*   **Limitations:** Sampling artifacts (stair-stepping, blurring) if slice spacing is too large or interpolation is poor. View-dependent artifacts in object-aligned modes. [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]
+*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization, medical imaging, legacy hardware. [S1, unofficial][S5, unofficial]
+*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity offline rendering, volumes with extreme anisotropy, or when view-independent sampling is critical. [S3, book]
 
 ## Background
-Texture-Based Volume Rendering was developed in the early 2000s to leverage the emerging 3D texture capabilities of graphics hardware (GPUs) for interactive visualization [S1, unofficial][S3, book]. It was popularized by works such as Krüger and Westermann (2003) and the NVIDIA GPU Gems Chapter 39 [S2, unofficial][S3, book]. The method evolved from earlier software-based ray casting and splatting techniques, addressing the need for real-time performance on consumer PC hardware [S2, S7, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical imaging visualization and scientific data exploration [S5, unofficial][S9].
+Texture-Based Volume Rendering was developed in the late 1990s and early 2000s as GPUs began to support 3D textures and fragment shaders. Key papers include Krüger and Westermann (2003) on GPU acceleration, and the NVIDIA GPU Gems Chapter 39 (2004) which detailed the implementation. It evolved from 2D texture slice approaches to 3D texture view-aligned slicing to address artifacts and memory issues. [S2, unofficial][S3, book][S7, unofficial]
 
